;;; Ein — feature demo
;;;
;;; The kernel trigger `(__symmetric__ R)` — the native arg-swap mirror.
;;;
;;; `(__symmetric__ R)` is a **dunder kernel trigger**, not a rule: the
;;; saturator closes R's extension under arg-swap directly (pop `(R a b)`,
;;; write `(R b a)`), skipping the JoinPlan + matcher the stdlib `symmetric`
;;; rule pays per mirror. Identical closure, one kernel fast-path —
;;; `docs/kernel/ir/03-ein-lang/06_reserved_names.md` and
;;; `utils/symmetric_bench.py` for the measurement.
;;;
;;; Contrast with `saturation/symmetric/*.ein`, which exercise the *userspace*
;;; `symmetric` rule from `std.algebra`. Nothing else in `examples/` marks a
;;; relation, so this file is the only fixture that reaches the mirror.
;;;
;;; THREE marked relations, on purpose. The mirror's cold seed walks the
;;; marked-relation set once, and that set is a `frozenset[str]`: before the
;;; M1a determinism audit (hazard H1 — `plans/m1a_rust/design/02` §5) the walk
;;; order was CPython's set order, so which relation got mirrored first — and
;;; hence the whole derivation order — depended on `PYTHONHASHSEED`. This file
;;; is the fixture that reproduces it: with one marked relation the bug is
;;; invisible, with three it shows up in `ein saturate --dump`'s "derived facts
;;; by relation" block. The seed is sorted now, so the firing sequence is a
;;; function of the input alone.
;;;
;;; The three relations are deliberately *unsorted* in the file (`knows`,
;;; `rivals`, `allies` — sorted: allies, knows, rivals), so a seed that
;;; happened to follow file order would not pass either.

(relation knows  Person Person)
(relation rivals Person Person)
(relation allies Person Person)
(relation is-a   T T)

(__symmetric__ knows)
(__symmetric__ rivals)
(__symmetric__ allies)

(is-a Ann  Person)
(is-a Bob  Person)
(is-a Cid  Person)
(is-a Dee  Person)
(is-a Eve  Person)
(is-a Fay  Person)

;; One edge per marked relation — each needs exactly one mirror, so the three
;; mirror firings are the whole derivation and their order is the observable.
(knows  Ann Bob)
(rivals Cid Dee)
(allies Eve Fay)

;; A self-loop and an already-mirrored pair: the two cases the mirror skips
;; (`a == b`) and records as an alternative justification rather than a new
;; fact (`(R b a)` already present).
(knows  Gil Gil)
(allies Hal Ivy)
(allies Ivy Hal)

;; After saturation the three marked relations are closed under arg-swap:
;;
;;   (knows  Bob Ann)   ; mirror of (knows  Ann Bob)
;;   (rivals Dee Cid)   ; mirror of (rivals Cid Dee)
;;   (allies Fay Eve)   ; mirror of (allies Eve Fay)
;;
;; and nothing else — `(knows Gil Gil)` is its own mirror (skipped), and the
;; `(allies Hal Ivy)` / `(allies Ivy Hal)` pair is already closed, so each
;; direction is recorded as a second justification of the other rather than
;; re-derived. `is-a` is unmarked and untouched.

(query
  :goal (knows Bob Ann)
)
